WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.901
Picture a checkout request on a payments site.

2
00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:07.641
It enters an API service.

3
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.584
The API calls a fraud check, which calls a card partner.

4
00:00:12.584 --> 00:00:15.023
The API also writes to a database.

5
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.725
The customer waited three seconds.

6
00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:20.073
Which hop took them?

7
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.614
Metrics give you averages per service.

8
00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:26.724
The 95th percentile of the fraud check went up.

9
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:30.800
Logs give you lines per service, each with its own timestamp.

10
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.413
You have to guess which lines belong to this one request.

11
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:38.331
Neither one tells you where this request spent its three seconds.

12
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:40.216
A trace does.

13
00:00:40.216 --> 00:00:44.900
It is the story of one request, told as a tree of timed steps.

14
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:46.449
Each step is a span.

15
00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:52.699
It has a name, a start time and an end time, so a duration, and some facts about what happened.

16
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.835
Every span in the trace carries the same trace ID.

17
00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:01.649
Each one also has its own span ID, and the span ID of its parent.

18
00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:03.693
The parent IDs make the tree.

19
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.851
The first span has no parent.

20
00:01:05.851 --> 00:01:07.668
It is called the root span.

21
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.668
But these spans are made in different services.

22
00:01:10.668 --> 00:01:14.143
Each service has to tell the next one which trace it is in.

23
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.152
That hand-over is context propagation.

24
00:01:17.152 --> 00:01:22.721
The caller injects the context into the outgoing request, as an HTTP header.

25
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:27.983
For HTTP, OpenTelemetry uses the W3C traceparent header.

26
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:31.139
It has four parts, split by dashes.

27
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:34.317
00 is the version of the format.

28
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:40.961
Next comes the trace ID: 32 hex characters, the same for every span of this request.

29
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:46.059
Then the parent span ID: 16 hex characters, the caller's current span.

30
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:47.521
Last, the flags.

31
00:01:47.521 --> 00:01:51.677
01 means sampled: the caller is recording this trace.

32
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.347
The receiver extracts the header, and starts its own span as a child of the caller's span.

33
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:02.136
Then it injects the same trace ID into its own outgoing call.

34
00:02:02.136 --> 00:02:04.833
Only the parent changes, hop by hop.

35
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:10.416
Trouble starts at a hop that does not pass it on, like a proxy that strips unknown headers.

36
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:15.706
The card partner then starts a new trace, and you get two traces that look unrelated.

37
00:02:15.706 --> 00:02:18.689
When a trace stops early, look for that hop first.

38
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.406
Each service exports its spans over OTLP, the OpenTelemetry Protocol.

39
00:02:25.406 --> 00:02:29.707
Most teams send them to an OpenTelemetry Collector first.

40
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:34.835
Receivers take data in.

41
00:02:34.835 --> 00:02:44.171
In the lab, the otlp receiver listens on 4317 for gRPC, and 4318 for HTTP.

42
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.880
Processors change it.

43
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.813
One hashes the client address, because an IP address is personal data.

44
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:52.518
Exporters send it out.

45
00:02:52.518 --> 00:02:57.105
Here, to Jaeger, which stores the traces and shows them in a web UI.

46
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:03.224
Jaeger draws the spans of one trace on a timeline, one row each.

47
00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:05.461
That picture is called a waterfall.

48
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.146
The API span covers all three seconds.

49
00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:09.973
The database write is short.

50
00:03:09.973 --> 00:03:13.431
The fraud check is long, but it has a child under it.

51
00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:17.895
The slow step is the longest bar with nothing underneath it.

52
00:03:17.895 --> 00:03:21.092
Here, the card partner: 2.6 of the 3 seconds.

53
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.036
It works for hello-devops too.

54
00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:29.736
In the lab, Redis writes were paused for 800 milliseconds, and one request was sent.

55
00:03:29.967 --> 00:03:37.077
GET / took 835 milliseconds, and its child span INCRBY took 832 of them.

56
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.720
So Redis is the slow step, and you know where to look next.

57
00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:47.099
One trace ID, carried in traceparent, joins the spans of every service into one waterfall.

58
00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:50.867
The longest bar with nothing under it is the slow step.
